# THE NVIM TUTORIAL · OVERVIEW

One course, zero to power user, built for YOUR exact setup — NvChad
2.5, your plugins, your custom mappings. Everything shown here works
on this machine. Start at chapter 1 even if you already know some
vim — it is condensed, and known exercises only take minutes.

## HOW THIS WORKS

- Every chapter is a real buffer. Practice directly on its text.
- Shaded lines are exercises. The sign column shows ✗ until your edit
  produces the exact expected result, then it flips to ✓.
- These buffers cannot be written. Break everything fearlessly;
  running `:Tutorial`{vim} again gives you a clean copy.
- Move the cursor onto a link and press `<Enter>`{normal} to follow
  it. Help links open `:help`{vim} pages — read them, that is where
  the real depth lives. Close a help window with `:q`{vim}.
- On a line that contains a command, type `>>`{normal} to run it.
  Try it on the line below:

    :echo "yes, >> works — you ran this from the tutorial"

## THE CHAPTERS

Do them in order. One chapter per sitting is plenty. Repeat a chapter
until every exercise is muscle memory, not a puzzle.

1. [The basics](@tutor:tutorial-01-basics) — modes, movement,
   editing, search, NvChad survival kit. Start here.
2. [Precision motions](@tutor:tutorial-02-motions) — put the cursor
   where the edit happens, in 1-2 keys
3. [Operators and text objects](@tutor:tutorial-03-operators) — the
   grammar that makes vim vim
4. [Registers](@tutor:tutorial-04-registers) — never lose a yank
5. [Search, substitute, global](@tutor:tutorial-05-search) —
   surgical bulk edits
6. [Macros](@tutor:tutorial-06-macros) — record once, apply
   everywhere
7. [Marks, jumps, buffers, windows](@tutor:tutorial-07-navigation) —
   fly around projects
8. [Visual block and number tricks](@tutor:tutorial-08-visualblock)
   — column edits and auto-numbering
9. [IDE moves on your setup](@tutor:tutorial-09-ide) — LSP,
   Telescope, gitsigns: everything VSCode did, but faster

## TRAINING RULES

1. No arrow keys. No mouse. If you catch yourself, undo and redo the
   move the vim way.
2. If you pressed the same motion three times in a row (jjj, wwww),
   stop — there was a better motion. Find it.
3. Think in sentences: verb + count + noun (`d`{normal} `2`{normal}
   `aw`{normal} = delete two words). Never "enter visual mode and
   wiggle" when a text object can say it directly.
4. After every change ask: could `.`{normal} repeat this? Edits that
   repeat with `.`{normal} are the fastest edits there are.
5. Turn on relative numbers while training: `:set rnu`{vim} (you
   have a toggle on `<leader>rn`{normal}). Counted jumps like
   `7j`{normal} become instant.

## DAILY DRILL (10 MINUTES)

- 2 min: reopen yesterday's chapter, redo its exercises at speed.
- 6 min: today's chapter.
- 2 min: in real code, consciously apply one new trick. One per day
  sticks; ten per day evaporate.

Ready: [Chapter 1 · The basics](@tutor:tutorial-01-basics)
